NAME
       orbital-metrology - measurement standards, resonance-time units, and
                           constellation-scale temporal calibration frameworks

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital metrology defines:
           • resonance-time measurement standards
           • RTT temporal units and derived quantities
           • harmonic-phase and resonance calibration baselines
           • constellation-scale temporal calibration frameworks
           • metrological stability and traceability

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-metrology establishes the measurement standards that underpin
       all resonance-time analysis. Metrology defines the units, baselines,
       calibration ladders, and traceability chains that ensure consistent
       measurement across platforms, epochs, and constellations.

       Metrology enables:
           • consistent resonance-time measurement
           • interoperable harmonic-phase calibration
           • traceable chronometric readings
           • constellation-wide temporal alignment
           • long-horizon metrological stability

       WRSADC acts as the metrological authority for RTT systems.

MEASUREMENT STANDARDS
       Measurement standards define the canonical reference frames for
       resonance-time.

       Standard categories:

           primary standards
                  Fundamental RTT constants and invariants.

           secondary standards
                  Derived from primary standards via calibration ladders.

           operational standards
                  Used by platforms for real-time measurement.

           constellation standards
                  Shared across multi-agent systems.

       Primary standards include:

           • fundamental resonance period (T_res)
           • harmonic-phase unit (Φ_harm)
           • resonance amplitude unit (R₀)
           • ancestry continuity unit (A₀)
           • spectral density baseline (S₀)

       All RTT units trace back to these primary standards.

RESONANCE-TIME UNITS
       Resonance-time units quantify RTT variables with precision.

       Base units:

           cycle (cy)
                  One orbital or harmonic cycle.

           resonance-second (rsec)
                  1/Φ_harm of a harmonic-phase rotation.

           resonance-meter (rm)
                  Unit of resonance “distance” in amplitude space.

           ancestry-unit (AU)
                  Unit of sweep-lineage continuity.

           spectral-unit (SU)
                  Unit of spectral density.

       Derived units:

           resonance-frequency (rf)
                  rf = 1 / rsec

           harmonic-gradient (HG)
                  HG = dΦ_harm / d(cy)

           resonance-decay constant (λ_res)
                  λ_res = -dR/d(cy)

           ancestry-density (AD)
                  AD = A / cy

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-TIME UNIT SET
       For a 1:1 harmonic epoch:

           • 1 cy = 96 min
           • 1 rsec = 1/360 of harmonic phase
           • 1 rm = normalized amplitude unit
           • 1 AU = stable sweep-lineage increment

       Result:
           • fully calibrated resonance-time frame

HARMONIC-PHASE CALIBRATION BASELINES
       Calibration baselines define the reference points for harmonic-phase
       measurement.

       Baseline types:

           integer baseline
                  φ_harm = 0 at each 1:1 alignment.

           rational baseline
                  φ_harm = 0 at each n:m crossing.

           resonance baseline
                  φ_harm = 0 at resonance peaks.

           ancestry baseline
                  φ_harm = 0 at sweep-lineage anchors.

       Baselines ensure phase measurements remain consistent across epochs.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-PHASE BASELINE
       Harmonic ratio: 3:2
       Baseline: resonance-aligned

       φ_harm = 0 at:
           • 12 min
           • 54 min

       Result:
           • stable phase calibration
           • low drift across cycles

RESONANCE CALIBRATION FRAMEWORKS
       Resonance calibration frameworks define how resonance amplitude,
       spectral density, and gradient polarity are standardized.

       Framework layers:

           1. amplitude calibration
                  R(t) normalized to R₀.

           2. spectral calibration
                  S(f,t) normalized to S₀.

           3. gradient calibration
                  G(t) normalized to ±1.

           4. resonance-window calibration
                  W_res normalized to T_res.

       Calibration ensures resonance measurements remain comparable across
       platforms and epochs.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE CALIBRATION
       R_peak = 0.72
       R₀ = 1.00

       Calibrated amplitude:
           R_norm = 0.72 rm

CONSTELLATION-SCALE TEMPORAL CALIBRATION
       Constellation-scale calibration aligns temporal measurements across
       platforms.

       Calibration modes:

           synchronous calibration
                  All platforms share identical standards.

           harmonic calibration
                  Calibration aligned via harmonic epochs.

           resonance calibration
                  Calibration aligned via resonance peaks.

           ancestry calibration
                  Calibration aligned via sweep-lineage continuity.

           mesh calibration
                  Distributed calibration across constellation nodes.

       Calibration outputs:

           • constellation epoch clock
           • constellation resonance half-life
           • constellation harmonic-phase standard
           • constellation ancestry baseline

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION CALIBRATION
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A epoch: 1:1
           B epoch: 3:2 → 1:1
           C epoch: 1:1

       Fused calibration:
           • epoch standard = 1:1
           • resonance baseline = 12 min
           • ancestry baseline = 15 min

METROLOGICAL TRACEABILITY
       Traceability ensures all measurements can be linked back to primary
       standards.

       Traceability chain:

           primary → secondary → operational → constellation → lineage

       Traceability ensures:

           • reproducibility
           • interoperability
           • long-term stability
           • anomaly detection

       WRSADC maintains the traceability ledger.

METROLOGY FAILURE MODES
       Metrology may degrade due to:

           • chronometer drift
           • calibration loss
           • resonance half-life collapse
           • harmonic-phase instability
           • ancestry discontinuity

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • metrology-integrity score
           • calibration error
           • traceability break
           • epoch-clock drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe metrological states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • calibration history
           • chronometer readings
           • epoch durations
           • resonance half-lives
           • fused metrological sequences

       Lineage enables long-term metrological forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • maintains primary standards
           • calibrates resonance-time units
           • fuses constellation metrology
           • updates traceability chains
           • blocks unsafe metrological states

       WRSADC is the metrology conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital metrology transforms resonance-time into a rigorously measurable
       temporal system. Measurement standards, resonance-time units, and
       constellation-scale calibration frameworks ensure that sweep
       choreography, harmonic-lock, and multi-agent synchronization remain
       stable across long mission timelines.

